<description>
The `JS_JSONStringify` function implements the `JSON.stringify` functionality in QuickJS. It takes a JavaScript value (`obj`) and converts it into a JSON string. The `replacer` argument can be a function or an array to filter the properties being stringified. The `space0` argument is used to control the indentation of the output string for pretty-printing.
</description>

<requirements>

<requirement>
The `ctx` argument must be a valid pointer to an initialized `JSContext`. This is because the function relies on the context for all its operations, including memory management and type checking.
</requirement>
<requirement>
The `obj` argument can be any valid `JSValue`. The function is designed to handle different Javascript types and convert them to their JSON string representation.
</requirement>
<requirement>
If the `replacer` argument is an array, its elements should be strings or numbers. The function iterates through the array and converts its elements to strings for property filtering.
</requirement>
<requirement>
The `space0` argument, if provided, should be a number or a string. If it's a number, its value will be clamped between 0 and 10 for indentation level, and if it's a string, it will be truncated to the first 10 characters.
</requirement>

</requirements>

